# pyAML Tools

This deployment carries a pyAML view for each model below. Each view is one
configuration the build wrote; nothing else in the deployment edits it.

{% for model in measured -%}
- `{{ model }}`: `data/pyaml/{{ model }}/configuration.yaml`{% if solves.get(model) == "single_pass" %} (single pass: no design simulator){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
---

## Measure through `pyaml_measure`

- Every measurement goes through `mcp__python__pyaml_measure`. Never step setpoints for a measurement in `mcp__python__execute`: pyAML writes there are refused.
- `pyaml_measure` steps the setpoints under the control target's run lock. Nothing is written back: a run that stops, fails or is interrupted leaves each setpoint where it stopped.
- Every call asks for approval. The prompt shows the model, the kind, the pyAML view's digest and every setpoint it steps; the measurement runs only on that view.
- Arguments:
  - `kind`: `orm`, `dispersion`, `trm`, `crm` or `chromaticity_monitor`.
  - `model`: the model; omit when only one model has a pyAML view.
  - `correctors`: corrector setpoint addresses, for `orm` only; omit for every corrector.
  - `modulation`: `bipolar` steps each setting to both sides of its present value, `unipolar` to one side; omit for bipolar, or for unipolar with `by: group`.
  - `by`: `magnet` steps one magnet per column, `group` steps the whole group as one column; `trm` and `crm` only; omit for magnet.
- A kind the model's measurement file does not allow is refused, naming the group or instrument it needs.

### Step sizes

- Each step comes from the model's measurement file: `corrector_delta`, `frequency_delta`, `quad_delta`, `sextu_delta`.
- The tool shrinks a step until no setpoint's largest single write exceeds half its `max_step` in `data/facility/limits.yaml`; the result names the setpoint that bounded it in `delta_limited_by` (`rf_limited_by` for the RF).
- A setpoint with no `max_step` in limits.yaml is stepped by the measurement file's value.

### A group stepped as one knob

- `by: group` moves every magnet of the group by the same integrated strength, once, from its present setting. Group stepping is unipolar: an omitted modulation is unipolar, and `modulation: bipolar` is refused.
- The group step is sized so the tune or chromaticity moves no more than under the strongest single-magnet step of the view's `trm.json` or `crm.json`, and no magnet moves by more than one magnet's step; then each member is held to half its `max_step`.
- `delta_basis` says what sized it: `tune excursion`, `chromaticity excursion`, `magnet step` or `max_step`.
- The group column is per unit integrated strength: the sum of its members' magnet-by-magnet columns.
- `crm` by group needs the view's `crm.json`, written whenever every member of the sextupole group has a modelled column; a group with an unmodelled member has none and refuses it.

### Bipolar or unipolar

- Bipolar is the default. The MATLAB Middle Layer measures tune and chromaticity responses unipolar because of magnet hysteresis: choose `unipolar` when a magnet must approach its setting from one side.

### The result

- `rows` and `columns` name each reading and each stepped setpoint by its address and device id; `deltas` gives each step by address, or by array name for a group.
- The matrix is in the file `data_file` names and in the artifact `artifact_id` names; the result carries its `shape`, never the matrix.
- A result with `completed` false stopped part-way: report every entry of `moved` (address, device, value before the call) and the `error` when there is one to the operator; those setpoints are where the run left them.
- A measurement that reaches the execution timeout is killed: its error names only the channels it attempted, without their values before the call. Read them and report them to the operator.
- `remainder` names what was not measured.

---

## Read the model through `model_response_matrix`

- `mcp__python__model_response_matrix(model, correctors)` solves a served model's orbit response on the model's own deck. It writes nothing and asks for no approval.
- Each corrector steps by the measurement file's `corrector_delta`, converted to the corrector's hardware unit; each entry is a readback change in its hardware unit per setpoint change in its own.
- `rows` and `columns` name each monitor readback and each corrector by address and device id.
- A solve longer than the executor's timeout is stopped and refused; name fewer correctors.

---

## Read through pyAML in `execute`

- Load a view in `mcp__python__execute`: `Accelerator.load("data/pyaml/<model>/configuration.yaml")` from `pyaml.accelerator`.
- `sr.live` reads the machine; `sr.design` computes on the design lattice and touches no hardware.
{% for model in measured if solves.get(model) == "single_pass" -%}
- `{{ model }}` is solved single pass: its view carries `simulators: []`, so it has no `sr.design`. Its reads are `sr.live` only.
{% endfor -%}
- Reads only: a pyAML write in `execute` is refused (`OspreyWriteRefused: pyAML writes run only inside pyaml_measure`).
- A named array: `sr.live.magnets.get("<array>")`, `sr.live.bpms.get("<array>")`. Never subscript a holder with a name: `sr.live.magnets["<array>"]` is a name glob and returns an empty array.
- Array values: `.strengths.get()`, `.positions.get()`; element names in order: `.names()`.
- The RF plant: `sr.live.rf.get("DEFAULT_RF_PLANT").frequency.get()`.
- The tunes: `sr.live.get_betatron_tune_monitor("BETATRON_TUNE").tune.get()`.
- pyAML values are SI; a channel reference's `[unit]` suffix names the unit the control system serves.
{% endif -%}
